  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above average temperatures today before a cold front \ngenerates a round of more substantial rain showers tonight \nthrough the first half of Friday.\n\nPrimarily fair weather is expected for the time being as a\nsubtle shortwave ridge moves generally E'ward across our CWA and\nis accompanied by stabilizing subsidence. However, ahead of this\nshortwave ridge, a shortwave trough axis will continue to move\nE'ward across Central NY through this late afternoon. Moist isentropic\nascent ahead of this shortwave trough axis will trigger isolated\nto scattered rain showers. Abundant cloud cover will limit the \ndepth of diurnal convective mixing of the boundary layer and the\nmixing's ability to tap into stronger flow aloft. However, the\nlimited mixing along with a tightening MSLP pressure gradient \nahead of an approaching cold front will elevate SW'erly surface\nwinds and gusts, especially northeast of the eastern Great \nLakes, where winds should gust up to 30 mph through this early \nevening, especially across the Niagara Frontier. Late afternoon \nhighs are expected to reach the 70's to 80F across most of \nWestern and Central NY, but mid 60's to mid 70's are expected in\nNorth- Central NY due to greater cloud cover and the later \ndeparture of the latest round of rain showers.\n\nA sharper trough will then dig across the upper Great Lakes forcing \nthe aforementioned cold front southeast through western and \nnorth-central NY overnight tonight through Friday morning. \nWhile PWAT values will be nearing the 100th percentile for this \ntime of year, along with a stout 35-45 knot SW'erly LLJ, the\ndistribution of rain showers will be mainly ana-frontal and \nthus not be able to take full advantage of the more plentiful \nmoisture and much stronger forcing for ascent along the surface\nportion of the front. Thus, while there will be a period of \nbeneficial showers, the heavy rainfall/flooding threat will be \nnear zero. The main window for more persistent showers will be \nlate tonight through midday/early afternoon Friday, just behind\nthe surface cold front and associated with moist isentropic\nascent along the upper-reaches of the front. A general one-third\nto two-thirds of an inch of rainfall is still expected. A few \nwidely scattered light showers may linger in the wake of the \ncold front Friday afternoon, otherwise dry and seasonable \nweather is on tap for the upcoming weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below-average temperatures with frost potential early next \nweek.\n\nA strong cold front with cross the area Sunday night, ushering in \nthe coolest airmass we've seen since last spring. 850 mb \ntemperatures should plunge to near 0C to -2C during the first \npart of next week, with daytime highs not getting out of the 50's\nfor much of the region, and possibly only upper 40's for highs \nacross the Tug Hill and western Dacks by Tuesday.\n \nThe axis of a strong upper-level trough axis will cross the \narea Monday, possibly producing a few showers, otherwise mainly \ndry weather is expected. The main impact will be frost potential\nMonday night, and possibly again Tuesday night, especially \nfarther inland from the eastern Great Lakes.",
